---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Chart from "../components/Chart.astro";
import { stateCpi, regional, asOf } from "../lib/data";
import { pct, number, asOfLabel } from "../lib/format";

const cpi = stateCpi();
const reg = regional();
const cpiAsOf = asOf(["cpi_state.json"]);

// The wages-period ranking drives the bars. Field names are mapped here rather than in the
// chart: this artifact block calls it `cumulative_pct` while the rankings use
// `cumulative_inflation_pct`, and a chart reading the wrong one renders "NaN".
const inflationRanking = (reg.meta.inflation_by_state_wages_period ?? []).map((r) => ({
  state: r.state,
  cumulative_inflation_pct: r.cumulative_pct,
}));

const divergenceRows = [
  ...reg.wages_ranking.map((r) => ({
    state: r.state,
    cumulative_inflation_pct: r.cumulative_inflation_pct,
    real_growth_pct: r.real_wage_growth_pct,
    measure: "Wages",
  })),
  ...reg.income_ranking.map((r) => ({
    state: r.state,
    cumulative_inflation_pct: r.cumulative_inflation_pct,
    real_growth_pct: r.real_income_growth_pct,
    measure: "Household income",
  })),
];

const w = reg.spreads.wages;
const i = reg.spreads.income;

// Correlation between inflation and real growth, computed here rather than asserted. A value
// near zero is the finding: the two are not linked.
function pearson(xs: number[], ys: number[]): number | null {
  const n = xs.length;
  if (n < 3) return null;
  const mx = xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / n;
  const my = ys.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / n;
  let num = 0;
  let dx = 0;
  let dy = 0;
  for (let k = 0; k < n; k++) {
    num += (xs[k] - mx) * (ys[k] - my);
    dx += (xs[k] - mx) ** 2;
    dy += (ys[k] - my) ** 2;
  }
  return dx && dy ? num / Math.sqrt(dx * dy) : null;
}

const wageCorr = pearson(
  reg.wages_ranking.map((r) => r.cumulative_inflation_pct),
  reg.wages_ranking.map((r) => r.real_wage_growth_pct),
);
const incomeCorr = pearson(
  reg.income_ranking.map((r) => r.cumulative_inflation_pct),
  reg.income_ranking.map((r) => r.real_income_growth_pct),
);

// State-level CPI series for the small-multiples comparison.
const stateOverall: Record<string, (number | null)[]> = {};
for (const state of cpi.meta.states) {
  stateOverall[state] = cpi.series[`${state}|overall`] ?? [];
}
---
<Base
  title="States — Malaysia Cost-of-Living Reality Check"
  description="Which states fell behind, and why. Cumulative inflation against real income growth for all 16 states and federal territories."
>
  <h1>States: the divergence is on the income side</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Price inflation is remarkably similar everywhere in Malaysia. Income growth is not. That
    asymmetry is the finding this whole project exists to show.
  </p>

  <div class="grid" style="margin:1.5rem 0">
    <div class="stat">
      <p class="label">Inflation spread</p>
      <p class="value">{number(w.inflation.spread_pp, 1)} pp</p>
      <p class="note">
        {w.inflation.min_state} {pct(w.inflation.min_pct, 1, false)} → {w.inflation.max_state}
        {" "}{pct(w.inflation.max_pct, 1, false)}, {reg.meta.wages_period.start_year}–{reg.meta.wages_period.end_year}
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <p class="label">Real wage growth spread</p>
      <p class="value">{number(w.real_growth.spread_pp, 1)} pp</p>
      <p class="note">
        {w.real_growth.min_state} {pct(w.real_growth.min_pct)} → {w.real_growth.max_state}
        {" "}{pct(w.real_growth.max_pct)}
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <p class="label">Correlation</p>
      <p class="value">{wageCorr === null ? "n/a" : number(wageCorr, 2)}</p>
      <p class="note">
        Between a state's inflation and its real wage growth. Near zero means higher prices did
        not come with higher income growth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Cumulative inflation by state</h2>
  <Chart
    name="state-inflation-bars"
    props={{ rows: inflationRanking }}
    title="Cumulative consumer price inflation, 2010 to latest"
    subtitle="The bars are close together. That is the whole point."
    height={440}
  />

  <h2>The two rankings, side by side</h2>
  <p>
    Sorted by real income growth. Note that the ordering here bears almost no relationship to
    the inflation ranking above: the state with the highest inflation is not the state that
    lost the most purchasing power.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Median wage by state, {reg.meta.wages_period.start_year}–{reg.meta.wages_period.end_year}
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Rank</th>
          <th scope="col">State</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Nominal growth</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Inflation</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Real growth</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Gap (nominal − inflation)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          reg.wages_ranking.map((r, idx) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{idx + 1}</td>
              <th scope="row">{r.state}</th>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.nominal_wage_growth_pct)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.cumulative_inflation_pct, 1, false)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.real_wage_growth_pct)}</td>
              <td class="num">
                {pct(r.nominal_wage_growth_pct - r.cumulative_inflation_pct)}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="data-note">
    The "gap" column is the simple subtraction, shown because it is the intuitive reading.
    It overstates the real change over a 15-year horizon: the figures above use the ratio of
    growth factors, which is correct. Chatting about the difference is a good interview
    question; the method is in <a href="/methodology">methodology</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The same picture from household income</h2>
  <p>
    A different survey, a different population, different years — and the same conclusion.
    Household real income growth, {reg.meta.income_period.start_year}–{reg.meta.income_period.end_year}:
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Household median income by state, {reg.meta.income_period.start_year}–{reg.meta.income_period.end_year}
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">State</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Nominal growth</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Inflation</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Real growth</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          reg.income_ranking.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.state}</th>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.nominal_income_growth_pct)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.cumulative_inflation_pct, 1, false)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.real_income_growth_pct)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Income growth spread: {number(i.real_growth.spread_pp, 1)} pp, against an inflation spread of
    just {number(i.inflation.spread_pp, 1)} pp. Correlation between the two across states:{" "}
    {incomeCorr === null ? "n/a" : number(incomeCorr, 2)}.
  </p>

  <Chart
    name="divergence-scatter"
    props={{ rows: divergenceRows }}
    title="Inflation against real income growth, both measures"
    subtitle="If prices drove the divergence, these would slope upwards. They do not."
    height={380}
  />

  <div class="callout warn">
    <h3>What this is not</h3>
    <p>
      There is no choropleth here. A map would imply a spatial pattern, and the data does not
      show one — neighbouring states land all over both rankings. A ranked bar chart is the
      honest rendering of the actual result. A map would be prettier and would suggest
      something the numbers do not support.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>Why we do not explain the gap</h3>
    <p>
      We can say the divergence is on the income side. We cannot say why from this data:
      industry mix, cost of living differences driving migration, oil revenue, urbanisation and
      minimum wage effects are all candidates, and none of them are testable with CPI and wage
      tables alone. Stating a cause here would be storytelling.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="data-note">
    <p>State CPI as of {asOfLabel(cpiAsOf)}</p>
    <p>
      Cumulative inflation figures use the mean of the 12 monthly index values in each calendar
      year. Partial years are excluded from the endpoints.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two periods are reported because the sources start in different years: wages
      {reg.meta.wages_period.start_year}–{reg.meta.wages_period.end_year}, household income
      {reg.meta.income_period.start_year}–{reg.meta.income_period.end_year}.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
